--TEST--
A loop that allocates nothing, writes nothing and calls nothing is still stopped by `[limits] cpu_time`, as the `FATAL` the tier-1 handler is told names `cpu_time` — `rule:errors/on-limit`, M6's *Verify*
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The cheapest attack there is: no allocation, no output, no member call, so
// every seam that notices a request has gone too far today is a seam this
// program never reaches. What stops it has to be the ceiling itself.
Core\Fatal::onLimit(fn (array<string> $report): void => {
    // `rule:errors/on-limit`'s `LimitReport`, whose `limit` key carries the
    // directive's own spelling — so the one thing a handler can do that reading
    // the message cannot is tell this breach from a memory one.
    echo "onLimit ", $report['limit'], "\n";
});
echo "start\n";

// The condition is a comparison rather than `true` so that nothing downstream
// may prove the loop never exits and treat what follows it as unreachable: the
// body is what this case is about, and it has to be compiled as written. An
// `int` step reaching the bound is 9,223,372,036,854,775,807 iterations away,
// which is not a way out of this loop on any machine.
int $spins = 0;
while ($spins >= 0) {
    $spins = $spins + 1;
}

// The first member call in the program after the loop, and it must not run: a
// request stopped at its ceiling is over where the ceiling was crossed, which
// is inside the body above.
echo "spun ", $spins, " times\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# 200 ms of CPU, of which `fatal_reserve_time`'s default 50 ms is the handler's
# slice — so ordinary execution gets 150 ms and the loop above crosses it in
# well under the case timeout.
[limits]
cpu_time = "200ms"
--EXPECT--
start
onLimit cpu_time
--EXPECT-ERROR--
FATAL: the request exceeded its CPU-time limit
